+\subsection{What remains unexplained}
+
+Ridge area was useful in two different ways. It exposed a strong association
+in the initial sample, and selection based on it enriched fresh pools.
+For regular pentagon products, the edge formula and capacity profile explain
+an inverse-square relationship. The sampled population is much broader:
+its association persists within every group, but the square--HKO comparison
+and opposing rotation paths rule out a universal monotonicity explanation.
+An explanation for the population pattern must account for the distribution
+of shapes as well as the symplectic areas of their faces.
+
+The search experiments also distinguish selection from deformation.
+A stronger filter did not always give a better selection. Equalizing support
+heights improved some bodies and harmed others. Rotations improved individual
+bodies but lost to fresh sampling under the tested allocation. Adapting the
+product distribution and retaining several local branches both helped in
+their respective comparisons. The endpoint tests show what the latter
+methods still missed: a basis poll could report no improvement even when
+nearby oblique directions allowed further ascent.
+
+None of the random samples, prospective selections, adaptive runs or
+matched-start optimizer runs produced a numerical ratio above one.
+The deliberately supplied HKO and pentagon controls are separate.
+For future above-one search, products with a triangle or quadrilateral
+factor are ruled out unless the deformation leaves the class covered by
+the theorem. The remaining product families and generic polytopes still
+present both questions pursued here: which geometric quantities explain
+capacity, and which changes in a body can increase its systolic ratio?
